schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: 'Custodial vs Non-Custodial Wallets: Understanding Who Holds the Keys'
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: comparison
  summary: >-
    Compares custodial wallets (a third party like an exchange holds your
    private keys, similar to a bank) against non-custodial wallets (you hold
    your own keys/seed phrase, no intermediary). Walks through the trade-offs
    of each — recovery, ease of use, DeFi access, privacy, counterparty risk —
    then gives East-Africa-specific framing (mobile money precedents like
    M-Pesa, Eva, Telesom ZAAD) and decision guidance for when to choose which.
  audience: Individual (beginner, East African communities)
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      ease-of-use-and-recoverability (custodial) vs full-ownership-and-no-safety-net
      (non-custodial) — the article frames this as situational, not a universal
      "better" choice.
    - >-
      convenience of built-in recovery vs the "not your keys, not your crypto"
      principle of true ownership.
  related_concepts:
    - custodial-wallet
    - non-custodial-wallet
    - seed-phrase
    - self-custody
  related_resources:
    - MetaMask
    - Ledger
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/custodial-vs-noncustodial.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-ce41b10364c7
  source_lineage: custodial-vs-noncustodial.md
  notes: >
    # assessed-but-excluded

    - Coinbase/Binance/Kraken and Trust Wallet/Rainbow/Trezor mentioned only as
      one-line named examples alongside MetaMask/Ledger; not built out as
      separate resource objects to avoid mega-listing named brands with no
      distinct content beyond "it's an exchange" / "it's a wallet app" — kept
      the two most-detailed examples (MetaMask, Ledger) as resources instead.
    - No people/community profiles, exact locations, or funding/legal/governance
      recommendations in this article; no high-risk trigger identified — it is
      general consumer wallet-literacy content, so no public-use-boundary
      object was created.
